WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.120
Germs are like cars.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.830
They are always on the move.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.890
And our hands? They're
like one big freeway,

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.820
moving germs toll-free
from location to location

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.690
outside and inside your body.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.550
But when you wash your hands,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.610
you create a major roadblock,

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.171
stopping the germs in their tracks.

00:00:19.171 --> 00:00:23.710
So, what would happen if you
gave up handwashing forever?

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.480
Your hands can have anywhere from 100,000

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.810
to a few million germs at any given time.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.680
Some of those germs live naturally

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.750
on your skin to protect you,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.848
but a lot of them are quite literally

00:00:35.848 --> 00:00:37.970
picked up in the environment.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.800
Those germs then sit,
waiting for transportation

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.715
to your mouth, nose, or eyes.

00:00:43.715 --> 00:00:48.000
And these environmental
germs can be downright evil.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.010
This was something we discovered
way back in the 1840s,

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.820
when Hungarian doctor
Ignaz Semmelweis noticed

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.890
that when doctors performed autopsies

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.120
and then helped women give birth,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.150
it led to a higher death
rate among the mothers.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.030
Semmelweis implemented
chlorine handwashing practices

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.300
throughout the hospital,

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.125
and death rates dropped significantly.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:11.960
Now, there are a lot of
disease-causing germs out there.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.401
Meet bacterial conjunctivitis,

00:01:14.401 --> 00:01:16.601
or, as you may know it, pink eye:

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:19.730
the pus-producing, eye-burning infection.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.790
Say you go to the bathroom
and don't wash up afterwards.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.505
That bacteria makes its
way onto your hands,

00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:27.390
and then a simple eye scratch will set

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.550
your pink eye into motion.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.436
After preparing uncooked
meat, petting certain animals,

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:35.070
or coming in contact with fecal matter,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.330
your stomach is about
to feel pretty terrible.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.950
Diarrhea-causing illnesses,
like salmonella or giardiasis,

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.755
are 30% more likely when
you don't wash your hands.

00:01:45.755 --> 00:01:47.560
And even after you recover,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.490
you are likely to find yourself catching

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.970
these illnesses again and again.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.610
As you continue touching
contaminated surfaces

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.370
and shaking hands, more
germs come into the mix.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:03.060
You are 20% more likely to
catch respiratory viruses,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.140
like influenzas, the common cold,

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.230
and, of course, COVID-19,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.320
potentially leading to complications

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.040
or even severe pneumonia.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.910
And then there's the norovirus.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.550
This severe stomach virus only needs

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.850
a single particle to infect you.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.220
Influenzas need 50 to 100.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.800
So without handwashing,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.803
you're almost guaranteed to
catch it sooner or later.

00:02:28.692 --> 00:02:31.037
Now, you could diminish
your chances of getting

00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:35.970
any of these diseases by
simply not touching your face.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.340
But good luck;

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.610
one small study found that on average,

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.500
people touch their faces
over 20 times an hour.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.670
And, sure, germs will die eventually

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.160
if they haven't been
transported to an opening,

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.540
but because you won't be
able to stop touching things,

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:53.680
you also won't be able

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.530
to stop picking up new
germs and other grime,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.400
so your hands will get
dirtier and dirtier,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.512
smellier and smellier.

00:03:01.512 --> 00:03:04.460
And chances are you aren't
moisturizing either,

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.550
which means your hands will become dry

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.950
and cracks will form, creating
a perfect pocket for germs.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.450
Let's say that now you
get a cut on your hand.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.070
Not only will all those diseases

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.060
have an even easier way inside,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.170
the most menacing germs might just join

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.770
this infection party.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.840
Staphylococcus, more
commonly known as staph,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.700
can be a relatively
harmless group of bacteria

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.530
and can even be commonly
found in some people's noses.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.040
But when a wound opens
up and staph moves in,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.420
expect skin irritations, boils, fevers,

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.660
and even blood infections.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.840
Some strains of staph and other bacteria

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.910
take it one step further.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.440
Welcome the flesh-eating bacteria,

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.970
also known for causing necrotizing
soft tissue infections.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.480
This life-threatening
infection is a result

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.490
of bacteria invading the skin,

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.300
often when a cut or wound is present.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.480
It can destroy the skin, muscle,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.380
and, if untreated, lead to death.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.520
So, you now have a cocktail
of infectious diseases,

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.050
severe blisters, rashes,

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.108
and your flesh is being eaten alive.

00:04:10.108 --> 00:04:12.360
It's right about now you
might want to rush off

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.790
and wash your hands.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.900
But remember, it's not
about how often you wash;

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.290
it's about how and when you wash.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.630
For how, make sure it's
for at least 20 seconds,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.900
covering every inch of your hand.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.940
If you don't have a sink or clean water,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.040
use at least 60% alcohol hand
sanitizer for 20 seconds,

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.580
making sure the hands stay wet throughout.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:36.030
And for when, wash whenever you think

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.882
you might have come in contact with germs.

00:04:38.882 --> 00:04:40.889
And try to see how long you can go

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:42.505
without touching your face.

